WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:05.339
- If you have a dream, if you have this crazy
idea, you must believe in it even if no one

00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:06.339
else does.

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:10.310
When you talk to people who have found the
work they were born to do, they are so excited

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:11.310
about it.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.740
You can tell that they have found something
that is really unique.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.250
I've never really been interested in living
a balanced life.

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:18.570
I think balanced people don't change the world.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:24.539
I didn't want to get old, and be like, "Oh
like 10 years ago, I had this idea "to do

00:00:24.539 --> 00:00:26.050
something but I never did anything about it."

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:30.690
We tend to focus on all of the fun things,
and the fun things are great but not everybody

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:33.390
wants to pay you to do those fun things.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:37.950
I'm a big fan of defining our aspirations,
and then crafting our life around them essentially.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:39.800
I actually don't think you need a niche.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.080
I don't believe that you have to have something
super specific.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:44.710
You have to connect with like-minded people.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:49.330
You have to surround yourself with people
who aren't necessarily doing the same things

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:52.060
that you're doing but they have the same attitudes
or the same mindset at least.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:55.100
She says, "People are always telling you "to
think outside the box."

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:57.300
She says, "My advice is to get in the box."

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:03.150
But because life is short, why not try to
work toward something that is not just okay

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:04.449
but is amazing?

00:01:04.449 --> 00:01:06.609
I'm a writer, traveler, and entrepreneur.

00:01:06.609 --> 00:01:11.359
I'm best known for going to every country
in the world, all 193, and for writing The

00:01:11.359 --> 00:01:13.590
Art of Non-conformity blog and books.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:15.500
My new book is called Born for This.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.880
It's all about how to find the work you were
meant to do.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:24.590
I'm Chris Guillebeau, and here are my top
10 rules for success.

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:29.630
If you have a dream, if you have this crazy
idea, you must believe in it even if no one

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:31.350
else does.

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:36.080
So when I had the idea to go to every country
in the world, that's what it felt like.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:37.490
It felt like this crazy idea.

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:40.850
It was like, "This just sounds totally ridiculous
and absurd," but I couldn't stop thinking

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:47.859
about it, and I realized that if I didn't
pursue it, I would always regret it, and that

00:01:47.859 --> 00:01:53.180
knowledge, that fear of regret drove to say
yes, and I talk to lots of other people who

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:55.299
use very similar language.

00:01:55.299 --> 00:02:00.560
There is a guy from Portland Maine whose crazy
idea was to walk across America, and he wasn't

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:01.959
even sure like, "How does that work?

00:02:01.959 --> 00:02:02.959
"How much time does it take?

00:02:02.959 --> 00:02:04.060
"Has anyone done this before?"

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:08.140
It was this crazy idea that wouldn't leave
him alone, and so one morning, he left his

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:15.320
home in Maine, and he started walking, and
7 1/2 months later, he arrived in San Francisco,

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:16.930
and I talked to him throughout the whole journey.

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:19.819
I talked to him in the beginning, and in the
middle, in the end, and I asked him, "What

00:02:19.819 --> 00:02:21.310
were the challenges like?"

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:22.490
And, "How did it go?"

00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:26.069
And he said, he said something that stayed
with me for the rest of my journey.

00:02:26.069 --> 00:02:30.310
He said, "You know, there are lots of external
challenges.

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:32.959
"It was hard on the body to get used to walking
that much.

00:02:32.959 --> 00:02:34.129
"Lots of things went wrong.

00:02:34.129 --> 00:02:36.989
"There were some emotional challenges as well."

00:02:36.989 --> 00:02:39.730
He just had a breakup, and he was processing
all of this.

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:45.230
He said, "But what I realized about two weeks
in, "once I stopped complaining "and rumbling

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:50.540
about the whole thing," he said, "I realized
that my task was pretty simple.

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:55.600
"All I had to do was walk essentially, "and
if I did nothing else, "if I got up every

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:00.540
morning, "and I made a little bit of progress,
"then eventually I would reach the goal, "eventually

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:05.410
I would arrive in San Francisco, "and achieve
that goal, reach that destination," and it

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:10.950
sounded really simple but it's stayed with
me for the final two years of my quest because

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:12.590
that's how I felt it was for me too.

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:17.379
All I had to do was get to 193 countries,
and I had my list of countries, and I was

00:03:17.379 --> 00:03:21.620
making progress, and if I got stuck somewhere,
eventually I'd have to overcome that challenge

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:27.290
but I could go and do some others first, and
I liked the logical, analytical progress of

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:28.290
it.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:30.599
It's something that related to me so just
keep going, you have to believe in your dream

00:03:30.599 --> 00:03:32.099
even if no one else does.

00:03:32.099 --> 00:03:36.610
I think a lot of people, when I talk to people
about this book, they were worried about mistakes

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:40.230
they had made, and they were worried about
like, "You know, I had this opportunity and

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:44.549
I let it go," or, "I should have done this,
"and I ended up doing something different."

00:03:44.549 --> 00:03:48.689
I really learned that you can recover from
most mistakes like most of us have made mistakes.

00:03:48.689 --> 00:03:53.150
We have done different things, and that's
normal so there are a lot of different paths.

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:56.939
I don't think there is just necessarily one
path for everyone.

00:03:56.939 --> 00:04:02.150
There is whole poem about the road not taken
or the road less traveled, and I took this

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:05.290
one and it made all the difference but we
don't really know the other story, right?

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:08.110
We don't know what happened like what if you'd
actually gone back and taken the other one?

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:09.519
Maybe it would have been great too.

00:04:09.519 --> 00:04:10.530
Maybe it would have been better.

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:14.681
So I do think there is more than one path
but I also think when you talk to people who

00:04:14.681 --> 00:04:18.380
have found the work they were born to do,
they are so excited about it.

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:22.600
You can tell that they have found something
that is really unique and really special.

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:27.169
So even though there is more than one path,
I think it's important to work toward whatever

00:04:27.169 --> 00:04:32.960
this special thing is, work toward this combination
of factors to where we're really happy, and

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:36.880
we have all the money that we need, and we're
doing something that we're really good at.

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:38.840
I think that's the goal, right?

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:43.340
So it's not like this one elusive thing but
if you look at really successful people, if

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:47.270
you look at Beyonce, Beyonce probably could
have done a bunch of different things but

00:04:47.270 --> 00:04:50.080
isn't it so good that she's doing what she's
doing?

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:51.479
Or anyone that you do admire.

00:04:51.479 --> 00:04:54.759
You look at them and you're like, "They have
really found their thing."

00:04:54.759 --> 00:04:57.820
I've never really been interested in living
a balanced life.

00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:00.699
I think balanced people don't change the world
first of all.

00:05:00.699 --> 00:05:03.550
I think balanced people don't really follow
a dream.

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:07.370
It doesn't mean that you don't have responsibilities
like just go to all the previous question,

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:08.669
"How can you have a family?"

00:05:08.669 --> 00:05:12.990
Sure, you can have a family.

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:15.380
I feel like balance is like a made-up word.

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:19.759
It's like invented by corporations to make
their employees think that they're happy.

00:05:19.759 --> 00:05:21.199
It's like you got this balance.

00:05:21.199 --> 00:05:23.719
You could work at Microsoft or something here
in Seattle.

00:05:23.719 --> 00:05:26.169
- [Chase] There is like 10 Microsoft people
in the audience in here.

00:05:26.169 --> 00:05:27.169
- That's right.

00:05:27.169 --> 00:05:28.169
- No disrespect to Microsoft.

00:05:28.169 --> 00:05:29.169
Let's pick another.

00:05:29.169 --> 00:05:30.169
Microsoft is a great company.

00:05:30.169 --> 00:05:32.070
- [Chase] One in every five people in Seattle
works at Microsoft.

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:33.070
- That's right.

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:36.660
And it's like you got this thing, and maybe
you love your job, and if you love your job,

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:41.009
that's great but I hear from a lot of dissatisfied
people at some of these big companies, and

00:05:41.009 --> 00:05:44.350
they write in, they're like, "I'm reading
your blog "from my cubicle, and like I had

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:49.360
this good job, "and I've got my health insurance,
I've got free yoga "every Thursday but I'm

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:50.360
not really--"

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:51.360
- Fulfilled.

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:52.360
- Right, "I'm not really fulfilled."

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:55.270
So for me, I guess I'm much more interested
in that word fulfilled.

00:05:55.270 --> 00:05:57.910
How can you live a fulfilled life?

00:05:57.910 --> 00:05:59.979
You can live a fulfilled life, and still pay
your bills.

00:05:59.979 --> 00:06:01.349
Lots of creative people do it.

00:06:01.349 --> 00:06:04.050
Lots of unconventional people do it.

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:07.539
I would say, if anything, it's actually much
more common now than it used to be.

00:06:07.539 --> 00:06:13.660
When I first started working online 15 years
ago, I didn't know a lot of other people who

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:14.729
were doing that.

00:06:14.729 --> 00:06:16.699
My friends thought I was selling drugs or
something.

00:06:16.699 --> 00:06:18.190
- [Chase] Well, you were.

00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:19.300
- I was but.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:21.070
Or like online gambling or whatever.

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:25.320
My parents have no idea how the internet works
and stuff but now it's much more common so

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:26.870
I don't really like the word balance myself.

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:34.000
- So what we'd like to do is we want to replace
the word, like living a balanced life is not

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:37.240
the goal, the goal is living a fulfilled life.

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:38.479
- I started traveling.

00:06:38.479 --> 00:06:39.870
I had been to a bunch of countries.

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:40.870
I really liked it.

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:44.789
I lived in West Africa for four years, and
started trying to figure out, "Okay, how many

00:06:44.789 --> 00:06:45.979
countries have I been to?

00:06:45.979 --> 00:06:47.800
"How many countries in the world are there?"

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:52.220
Once I had this information, it was very difficult
to do anything else, right?

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:56.710
Because once I had this information, I was
like, "Oh I've been to like 50 countries.

00:06:56.710 --> 00:06:57.710
"There's 193 countries.

00:06:57.710 --> 00:06:58.710
"It was 192.

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:00.583
"I would like to visit 100 countries some
time in my life."

00:07:00.583 --> 00:07:02.220
I set that goal first.

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:06.449
- But you just said, you say, "I would like
to "'cause I would like to do a lot of things--"

00:07:06.449 --> 00:07:07.449
- No no, I want to.

00:07:07.449 --> 00:07:08.500
I feel drawn to it.

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:13.260
I feel like this is a goal attached to myself,
and at the time, it wasn't public.

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:14.680
It was just like a private thing.

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:16.360
This is before I was blogging.

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:20.230
And then as I started getting closer to that
goal of 100 countries, that's when I thought,

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:21.230
"What's next?"

00:07:21.230 --> 00:07:24.490
Because if you're only going to half the countries,
then you're missing a lot of challenges, and

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:25.780
you could just go to easy places.

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:32.110
And so I had this information, I was like,
"Could I really go to every country?"

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:33.650
I don't know if I could or not.

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:34.909
This is a huge thing.

00:07:34.909 --> 00:07:39.110
It's going to cost a lot, not just in terms
of money but also in terms of time and sacrifice

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:40.289
and logistics.

00:07:40.289 --> 00:07:41.819
Things could go wrong, fear.

00:07:41.819 --> 00:07:46.850
But really what it came down to for me was
not the fear of something going wrong along

00:07:46.850 --> 00:07:51.560
the way, once I actually started thinking
about this, I was like, "Maybe I could actually

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:54.669
do this in seven to eight years if I really
worked at it.

00:07:54.669 --> 00:07:56.960
Then what I was afraid of was not doing it.

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:03.090
I was afraid of having this idea, and then
just passing on it, and just continuing to

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:04.129
travel a little bit.

00:08:04.129 --> 00:08:07.479
I'm that guy who travels once in a while but
what's that?

00:08:07.479 --> 00:08:13.450
And then I didn't want to get old, and be
like, "Oh 10 years ago, I had this idea "to

00:08:13.450 --> 00:08:16.460
do something but I never did anything about
it."

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:17.460
That's what drove me.

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:19.569
- The fear of being ordinary or if not--

00:08:19.569 --> 00:08:23.639
- The fear of regret, of not following up
on a dream that I had, that I knew, I didn't

00:08:23.639 --> 00:08:28.189
know if I could succeed, I thought it might
fail but I figured if I failed, I could deal

00:08:28.189 --> 00:08:31.370
with that but not if I just didn't do it.

00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:32.370
- Right.

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:33.370
- [Chris] That's what drove it.

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:38.229
- For those people who've heard your story,
and want to live a non-conformist traveling

00:08:38.229 --> 00:08:42.140
lifestyle as well, could you recommend a business
model we start out with?

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:44.320
We tried info products, we tried eBay.

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:45.980
What do you recommend?

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:49.750
- Dude, the most important thing I recommend
regardless of what the specific business model

00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:55.500
is is to really focus on what I call the convergence
between what you want to do, and what you're

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:59.560
passionate about, and what other people are
also passionate about, and what other people

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:04.070
are actually willing to exchange money for
because those things can be different sometimes.

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:09.710
I think often when we focus on like vocation
independence and doing what you want stuff,

00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:14.461
and we tend to focus all of the fun things,
and the fun things are great but not everybody

00:09:14.461 --> 00:09:20.050
wants to pay you to do those fun things so
you have to find a link between what you can

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:24.220
do that's really helpful, where your skills
are, how your passions can align with other

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:27.630
people, and that took me quite a while to
figure it out but if you really focus on that

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:31.620
from the beginning, then I think that helps
regardless of the specific business model.

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:35.600
Now in terms of the business model, right
now I'm in the information publishing business.

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:36.600
I really do love it.

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:42.110
I think it's great but there's other things
you can do as well so it just depends on what

00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:46.130
you're into and what kind of business you
want to pursue I think.

00:09:46.130 --> 00:09:48.970
- We all have these great ideas, right?

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:53.690
And at the same time, we have responsibilities
and mortgages and whatever so I guess I'm

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:59.230
just wondering what your advice is to people
who want to go out and pursue certain things

00:09:59.230 --> 00:10:05.279
but there are also certain limitations I guess
that we have to exist within.

00:10:05.279 --> 00:10:10.910
I guess we don't but you know, I can't walk
away from my condo tomorrow and walk across

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:11.910
the country.

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:14.720
It probably wouldn't work out well for me
but yeah, what's your advice?

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:17.190
- That's a great question but do you want
to walk across the country?

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:19.700
Is that your aspiration?

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:26.510
So I'm a big fan of defining our aspirations,
and then crafting our life around them essentially,

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:29.500
and so the quest to visit every country in
the world for example, I was thinking about

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:30.500
this the other day.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:32.680
People ask how much it cost, right?

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:36.670
And the first 100 countries cost about $30,000.

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:40.820
So 30,000 at least I would argue, is not a
huge amount of money for going to 100 countries,

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:45.209
and all the life experience that I got through
that, and also I didn't have to pay it all

00:10:45.209 --> 00:10:46.260
right away.

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:52.329
You pay as you go essentially and something
like that so you know, these limitations as

00:10:52.329 --> 00:10:55.680
you said, maybe let's not call them limitations,
they're responsibilities especially people

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:56.680
with families.

00:10:56.680 --> 00:11:01.339
You don't just leave your family, and walk
across the country but again, there are ways

00:11:01.339 --> 00:11:03.220
to cultivate the value of adventure.

00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:05.950
That's why I like the story of Sasha and what
she did at home.

00:11:05.950 --> 00:11:10.090
There is a woman in Omaha who's knitting 10,000
hats, and her passion, the thing that she

00:11:10.090 --> 00:11:14.560
really loved was always knitting, and one
Christmas time or holiday time, she made 100

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:17.750
hats for her friends and family, and she was
like, "That's really fun.

00:11:17.750 --> 00:11:20.980
"I want to do 1,000," but 1,000 is not a big
enough challenge so she took on this goal

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:24.470
of 10,000 hats, and there is a charity component
to it.

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:28.589
You can connect with her on her website, and
eventually get a hat.

00:11:28.589 --> 00:11:29.730
I don't know.

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:33.430
Responsibilities and things are real but if
something matters to us, I guess I'm also

00:11:33.430 --> 00:11:36.290
a believer in figuring out how to do that,
right?

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:40.360
Like we do have to trade off things, we do
have to sacrifice, and especially for all

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:45.891
of us who have so many opportunities available
to us, it's a matter of prioritization, it's

00:11:45.891 --> 00:11:47.720
a matter of.

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:51.389
I can probably do anything I want but I can't
do everything, right?

00:11:51.389 --> 00:11:55.460
So if this thing really does matter to me,
whether it's walking across the country or

00:11:55.460 --> 00:12:00.260
whatever it is in your case, then it's something
that you have to plan for and build for, and

00:12:00.260 --> 00:12:02.299
for me, it was worth it to do that.

00:12:02.299 --> 00:12:07.560
- When you're starting a blog or trying to
build an audience, how important is it to

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:11.589
have a niche, and what can you do if you have
multiple interests?

00:12:11.589 --> 00:12:17.589
- You find a way to meld the different interests,
you find a way, so I'm going to paraphrase

00:12:17.589 --> 00:12:23.149
a little bit, to put them together, and the
word that I like to use is not niche or niche,

00:12:23.149 --> 00:12:27.180
I like to use the word theme so I actually
don't think you need a niche.

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:29.760
I don't believe that you have to have something
super specific.

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:34.040
I know that there are some people who say
that, and maybe that's been true for them.

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:35.040
That's great.

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:39.880
In my case, it certainly wasn't the way that
I chose to do it, and partly that was because

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:44.160
I was worried I would get bored if it was
something too specific.

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:48.440
I had started a lot of projects in the past
where I was really motivated and excited for

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:52.820
a while but then it dropped off, and with
this one, when I started writing the blog,

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:56.480
it was something that I really liked the medium,
and I wanted to be able to keep doing it for

00:12:56.480 --> 00:13:01.320
a long period of time so I didn't choose a
niche but I did, as Dave referred to, I did

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:05.970
meld the interests together, and I chose a
theme, and I said, "What do all these things

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:07.340
have in common?"

00:13:07.340 --> 00:13:13.829
It's more of a world view, non-conformity
than it is anything super specific.

00:13:13.829 --> 00:13:15.459
You have to connect with like-minded people.

00:13:15.459 --> 00:13:20.070
You have to surround yourself with people
who aren't necessarily doing the same things

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:23.550
that you're doing but they have the same attitudes
or the same mindset at least.

00:13:23.550 --> 00:13:28.510
So that's another thing that has been transformative
because now when I meet readers or I come

00:13:28.510 --> 00:13:32.949
to do things like this, I find it, I come
in this weird business of inspiration, right?

00:13:32.949 --> 00:13:38.389
And people are like, "Oh so inspiring," but
it's totally true that I legitimately drive

00:13:38.389 --> 00:13:43.310
a great deal of my inspiration from the feedback
and interaction as you were saying with Twitter

00:13:43.310 --> 00:13:45.580
so online and offline.

00:13:45.580 --> 00:13:47.010
Online is great.

00:13:47.010 --> 00:13:50.581
Relationships can be forged online, and you
actually meet that person, and you feel like

00:13:50.581 --> 00:13:52.860
you know them already and stuff.

00:13:52.860 --> 00:13:56.829
When I do stuff like this, part of the reason
we do the World Domination Summit is to bring

00:13:56.829 --> 00:14:01.529
together people who are like-minded especially
if they come from small towns or something,

00:14:01.529 --> 00:14:05.269
and I hear this a lot of like, "I live in
this place "where it's not like Seattle or

00:14:05.269 --> 00:14:07.029
Portland or whatever.

00:14:07.029 --> 00:14:11.620
"There is just not a lot of people "who think
like I do," or, "My ideas aren't accepted,"

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:16.660
or, "My family doesn't understand why I want
to be an artist "or why I want to travel."

00:14:16.660 --> 00:14:18.930
And it's just like, "Why don't you just go
on vacation?

00:14:18.930 --> 00:14:21.100
"Why don't you "go and see the world?"

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:26.550
So bringing people together, and then just
myself, by being in that, it's helped so,

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:27.550
so much.

00:14:27.550 --> 00:14:30.620
I guess the general principle is I am pro-goal,
right?

00:14:30.620 --> 00:14:33.440
I am pro-parameters.

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:39.410
There is this good story in the book about
a woman named Elise Blaha Cripe who is a serial

00:14:39.410 --> 00:14:42.880
crafter, and she makes all this stuff, and
she shares it with the world, and she's incredibly

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:47.990
prolific, and her whole advice is she says,
"People are always telling you to think outside

00:14:47.990 --> 00:14:48.990
the box."

00:14:48.990 --> 00:14:52.420
She says, "My advice is to get in the box
basically, "and make yourself a box," like

00:14:52.420 --> 00:14:54.980
make yourself these parameters, these goals,
whatever.

00:14:54.980 --> 00:14:57.370
Again, it's not something that's being imposed
on you.

00:14:57.370 --> 00:14:58.389
That's really the clear point.

00:14:58.389 --> 00:15:00.970
It's not like someone is saying, "Here is
your goal, "and here is what you should do,"

00:15:00.970 --> 00:15:05.830
it's you, it's the things that you select
for yourself but I believe there is so much

00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:11.079
possibility in those parameters or in those
constraints as we talked about so whether

00:15:11.079 --> 00:15:15.030
it's like, "My goal is to climb Mount Everest,"
or maybe the 10 mountains or something, and

00:15:15.030 --> 00:15:20.250
in stages are also good so stages are milestones,
the levels in Mario Brothers.

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:24.970
For me, it was like 50 countries, 100 countries,
then it was like every continent.

00:15:24.970 --> 00:15:27.740
Once I finished all of Africa, that was a
big thing.

00:15:27.740 --> 00:15:31.870
So I do think it's important to celebrate
small wins along the way.

00:15:31.870 --> 00:15:35.800
Everybody who is successful, and by successful,
I mean like absolutely fulfilled in their

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:38.950
career, passionate about it, and feeling like
they're doing something that they're supposed

00:15:38.950 --> 00:15:44.819
to do, they have these three characteristics,
and first of all, they have joy, they're happy

00:15:44.819 --> 00:15:49.740
in their work, they enjoy what they do most
of the time, they're not doing a soul-sucking

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:50.740
job.

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:53.640
If they have a job, it's a great job, or if
it's something that they built for themselves,

00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:54.810
they really enjoy it.

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:58.920
And the second quality is money is because
there's lots of things you can do that you

00:15:58.920 --> 00:16:02.620
enjoy but if you don't get paid for them,
then it's a hobby, which is great, it's good

00:16:02.620 --> 00:16:07.170
to have a hobby but if you're talking about
a career, it has to be financially sustainable.

00:16:07.170 --> 00:16:11.370
So joy, money, and then the third factor is
flow, which is really doing something that

00:16:11.370 --> 00:16:16.700
you're good at, really doing something where
when you do it, you're immersed in it, and

00:16:16.700 --> 00:16:21.011
the hours just pass by, and it's also really
easy for you 'cause it's something that you're

00:16:21.011 --> 00:16:24.529
good at, and other people are like, "Wow,
she's really good at that.

00:16:24.529 --> 00:16:28.040
"It takes me a lot longer to do that," or,
"I'm not good at it," but so I think what

00:16:28.040 --> 00:16:30.490
we're looking for is this combination of all
three factors.

00:16:30.490 --> 00:16:34.680
We want something that brings joy, we want
something that is financially sustainable,

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:38.170
we want something that brings flow, and I
don't think people will be happy unless they

00:16:38.170 --> 00:16:39.700
have all three of these factors.

00:16:39.700 --> 00:16:41.820
I think you can be okay, right?

00:16:41.820 --> 00:16:46.880
There is lots of people who do jobs they don't
love, and that's fine, they get a paycheck,

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:51.101
and maybe they're satisfied in other areas
of their life but because life is short, why

00:16:51.101 --> 00:16:56.680
not try to work toward something that is not
just okay but is amazing, right?

00:16:56.680 --> 00:16:58.079
Isn't that the goal?

00:16:58.079 --> 00:17:02.200
- So those are the top 10 rules but since
we have you here live, I'd love to hear a

00:17:02.200 --> 00:17:04.380
bonus 11th rule from you.

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:05.380
- Okay.

00:17:05.380 --> 00:17:06.380
All right, no pressure.

00:17:06.380 --> 00:17:09.890
Bonus 11th rule is if something isn't working,
I think you should stop doing it.

00:17:09.890 --> 00:17:11.000
I think you should quit.

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:13.800
And this runs contrary to a lot of things
that you've been told.

00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:17.140
In your business education, through case studies,
et cetera, you've probably been told that

00:17:17.140 --> 00:17:21.010
winners never quit, and what I saw through
my research, looking at lots of successful

00:17:21.010 --> 00:17:24.310
people who've done a lot of amazing things,
winners actually aren't afraid to quit, winners

00:17:24.310 --> 00:17:28.800
quit all the time, and so I think it's not
persistence that's the predictor of success,

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:30.380
it's flexibility and adaptation.

00:17:30.380 --> 00:17:33.040
Being willing to go down a path and say, "Actually
that's not the right path for me.

00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:35.860
"I'm going to go back and then take the other
path "or I'm going to create an all-new path."

00:17:35.860 --> 00:17:38.900
So winners never quit maybe but winners actually
give up all the time.

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:42.570
- And how do you know, and here is the magic
question, how do you know when to quit and

00:17:42.570 --> 00:17:46.060
when to keep going 'cause this is still your
dream and this is really hard?

00:17:46.060 --> 00:17:47.060
- Absolutely.

00:17:47.060 --> 00:17:48.060
Here is the magic answer.

00:17:48.060 --> 00:17:49.290
You ask yourself two questions.

00:17:49.290 --> 00:17:50.620
Is it working?

00:17:50.620 --> 00:17:51.790
Do I still believe in it?

00:17:51.790 --> 00:17:56.700
So if it's not working but you still believe
in it, then you have to change what you're

00:17:56.700 --> 00:17:58.640
doing, you have to adapt because it's not
working.

00:17:58.640 --> 00:18:01.661
You have to recognize the reality but if you
still believe in your dreams, you have to

00:18:01.661 --> 00:18:02.750
change it.

00:18:02.750 --> 00:18:05.620
If it's not working, you don't believe in
it, then you stop and you quit.

00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:09.490
I would also say if it's actually working
okay but you no longer believe in it, you

00:18:09.490 --> 00:18:11.830
have to make a change there too 'cause ultimately
you're not going to be fulfilled doing something

00:18:11.830 --> 00:18:12.830
you don't care about.

00:18:12.830 --> 00:18:16.630
- And what would you tell people who don't
know what they believe in yet?

00:18:16.630 --> 00:18:18.170
I guess read the book.

00:18:18.170 --> 00:18:19.170
- Oh sure.

00:18:19.170 --> 00:18:25.190
- There is a lot of anxiety around finding
the perfect job for me, the perfect business.

00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:27.670
How do I go about finding something to actually
believe in?

00:18:27.670 --> 00:18:29.000
- Yeah, here are some reassurance.

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:30.910
Most people actually have no idea what it
is.

00:18:30.910 --> 00:18:34.460
Most people do not know their life purpose
when they're 20 or even 30 or older.

00:18:34.460 --> 00:18:37.550
What most successful people do is they go
down a lot of different paths.

00:18:37.550 --> 00:18:39.060
They do not follow a linear path.

00:18:39.060 --> 00:18:42.990
They aren't actually driven from a young age
to be this profession or whatever, they experiment,

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:47.810
they aren't afraid to go back as I said but
over time, they refine, over time, they discover

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:49.730
that but if you don't experiment, you won't
discover it.

00:18:49.730 --> 00:18:50.730
- I love it.

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:52.120
So thank you, guys, so much for watching.

00:18:52.120 --> 00:18:54.000
- Hey, thanks a lot, Jay Rise.

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:55.410
I heard you nominated me to be on the program.

00:18:55.410 --> 00:18:56.780
I really appreciate that, man.

00:18:56.780 --> 00:18:57.780
Thank you.

00:18:57.780 --> 00:19:01.360
- I'd love to know which of the top 10 rules,
or 11 rules I guess here, you guys like the

00:19:01.360 --> 00:19:02.360
most.

00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:04.370
Leave it in the comments below, and I'll join
in the discussion.

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:08.550
Now usually I throw it over to you guys for
your suggestions on who to profile next but

00:19:08.550 --> 00:19:13.291
since I have Chris here with me, if you had
to pick a few people for who do want to see

00:19:13.291 --> 00:19:14.960
a profile of, who would you pick?

00:19:14.960 --> 00:19:15.960
- Right, right.

00:19:15.960 --> 00:19:17.760
Obviously it's an amazing show so I got to
think of amazing people.

00:19:17.760 --> 00:19:19.900
First person I thought of is my good friend
Jonathan Fields.

00:19:19.900 --> 00:19:20.960
He lives in New York City.

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:22.050
He's done a lot of different things.

00:19:22.050 --> 00:19:24.760
He's probably most known for the Good Life
project now.

00:19:24.760 --> 00:19:26.040
Fantastic video series.

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:29.220
Michelle Poler, also in New York although
she's originally from Venezuela.

00:19:29.220 --> 00:19:33.850
She did 100 Days of Fear project where every
day, she made a different video all about

00:19:33.850 --> 00:19:34.850
fears she was overcoming.

00:19:34.850 --> 00:19:35.850
Really, really good stuff.

00:19:35.850 --> 00:19:39.920
And then the third person I'm thinking of
is Barbara Sher, and Barbara Sher is a little

00:19:39.920 --> 00:19:40.920
bit older.

00:19:40.920 --> 00:19:42.810
She's been writing books for at least 30,
40 years.

00:19:42.810 --> 00:19:47.260
A lot of people who are new get credit for
this lifestyle design movement but she's actually

00:19:47.260 --> 00:19:48.440
been doing it for a very, very long time.

00:19:48.440 --> 00:19:51.420
She wrote a book called Wishcraft like W-I-S-H.

00:19:51.420 --> 00:19:56.460
Wishcraft, that influenced me a lot.

00:19:56.460 --> 00:19:57.980
- So thank you, guys, so much for watching.

00:19:57.980 --> 00:20:01.170
Make sure you pick up a copy of Born for This.

00:20:01.170 --> 00:20:10.720
Continue to believe, and we'll see you soon.

00:20:10.720 --> 00:20:11.880
- I like a quote by Seth Godin.

00:20:11.880 --> 00:20:15.860
He said, "Effort can be its own reward if
you let it."

00:20:15.860 --> 00:20:17.550
So we're talking about motivations.

00:20:17.550 --> 00:20:23.470
I think it's critical to be motivated by doing
the work itself, that the work itself is enjoyable,

00:20:23.470 --> 00:20:25.450
maybe not each aspect of it, right?

00:20:25.450 --> 00:20:28.770
But it has to be because as you said, you
can tell, you can see drafts and iteration,

00:20:28.770 --> 00:20:34.651
you can see like a person's gone through something,
and I guess for me, once I was like, and once

00:20:34.651 --> 00:20:38.200
I actually found a little bit of process,
I said, "Okay, I'm going to write this blog

00:20:38.200 --> 00:20:40.400
post, "and then write books and things."

00:20:40.400 --> 00:20:44.030
I was like, "This is what I want to do."

00:20:44.030 --> 00:20:45.030
It's great.

00:20:45.030 --> 00:20:46.650
It's wonderful that people care about it now.

00:20:46.650 --> 00:20:51.480
it's great that so many people do but I'm
pretty sure I would do it anyway, and I said

00:20:51.480 --> 00:20:56.150
in the beginning, I was like, "I just want
to put this out for those who care," so for

00:20:56.150 --> 00:21:00.700
me, I'm very motivated by, we talked about
deliverables earlier.

00:21:00.700 --> 00:21:02.460
Every day, I'm trying to write 1,000 words.

00:21:02.460 --> 00:21:04.770
This is an actionable habit--

00:21:04.770 --> 00:21:06.300
- Yeah, I want to get into that.

00:21:06.300 --> 00:21:07.460
We made some notes about this.

00:21:07.460 --> 00:21:08.600
What are these tactics?

00:21:08.600 --> 00:21:09.600
- Tactics.

00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:13.840
For me, I said I'd write 1,000 words a day,
and I'm always traveling and doing lots of

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:14.840
different stuff.

00:21:14.840 --> 00:21:16.980
My goal is every day, I'm going to write 1,000
words.

00:21:16.980 --> 00:21:19.580
A lot of it is not going to be good, some
of those are not going to be usable but that's

00:21:19.580 --> 00:21:20.580
all right.

00:21:20.580 --> 00:21:21.580
I'm just trying to create.

00:21:21.580 --> 00:21:22.580
- [Chase] Every day?

00:21:22.580 --> 00:21:23.580
Seven days?

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:25.740
- The way I think of it is like exercise.

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:27.260
If I miss a day or something, it's okay.

00:21:27.260 --> 00:21:30.090
If I miss a couple days, I start feeling it.

00:21:30.090 --> 00:21:32.030
So like five to six days a week basically.

00:21:32.030 --> 00:21:33.030
- That's amazing.

00:21:33.030 --> 00:21:34.570
Anybody out there in the audience--

00:21:34.570 --> 00:21:35.780
- [Chris] It's not that hard.

00:21:35.780 --> 00:21:37.870
It's amazing but let's pull that back 'cause
I don't think it's that hard.

00:21:37.870 --> 00:21:40.310
- Right 'cause you're just sitting down and
doing the work.

00:21:40.310 --> 00:21:47.481
Chuck Close, a famous artist, said, "If I
only painted or if I only went to work "when

00:21:47.481 --> 00:21:50.120
I was inspired, I wouldn't have done very
much work."

00:21:50.120 --> 00:21:53.710
There is a process of getting up every morning,
and sitting down and writing.

00:21:53.710 --> 00:21:57.860
- Right, inspiration is like sitting down
to do it, you know?

00:21:57.860 --> 00:22:01.280
Jack London, it's like inspiration doesn't
find you, you find it with a club or something.

00:22:01.280 --> 00:22:03.640
You got to go after that inspiration, you
know?

00:22:03.640 --> 00:22:04.640
- I love that.

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:05.640
I love that.

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:06.640
- So 1,000 words.

00:22:06.640 --> 00:22:07.640
- 1,000 words.

00:22:07.640 --> 00:22:10.640
These are tactics that a writer, that if you
want to write, you should do every day.

00:22:10.640 --> 00:22:11.990
Get up and write 1,000 words.

00:22:11.990 --> 00:22:15.990
- Focus on outcomes and deliverables, not
focus on time schedule.

00:22:15.990 --> 00:22:20.310
What I mean by that is I often see advice
that's like, "Okay, you're going to set a

00:22:20.310 --> 00:22:23.040
timer or something, "and you're going to work
on this project "from like eight o'clock to

00:22:23.040 --> 00:22:26.740
8:50," or something like that, and that works
for some people and that's great but for me,

00:22:26.740 --> 00:22:30.420
I'm like always in different timezones, lots
of stuff is happening so I'm only focused

00:22:30.420 --> 00:22:31.900
on what the actual product is.

00:22:31.900 --> 00:22:37.580
So a product is like okay, blog post, manuscript,
or something, interview, email, or something

00:22:37.580 --> 00:22:38.680
so I'm always focused on shipping.

00:22:38.680 --> 00:22:42.130
- [Chase] There is an objective, shipping
pieces of it.

00:22:42.130 --> 00:22:43.130
- Shipping.

00:22:43.130 --> 00:22:48.280
Shipping, deliverables, outcomes, and just
like that quote, "Effort can be its own reward,"

00:22:48.280 --> 00:22:49.400
I find that enjoyable.

00:22:49.400 --> 00:22:53.450
I find the process of sending something out,
or pressing the publish button or whatever,

00:22:53.450 --> 00:22:56.530
I find that enjoyable, and so that's the reward
that I'm working for.

00:22:56.530 --> 00:23:00.260
I'm not looking for the reward of like the
viral blog post, I'm not looking for the reward

00:23:00.260 --> 00:23:03.300
of like lots of people buying something or
whatever.

00:23:03.300 --> 00:23:04.700
That's great but those are by-products.

00:23:04.700 --> 00:23:07.830
So I'm looking for the reward of getting it
out.

00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:15.880
- So the reward is in the act, and the creative
act itself, and if the reward is not in the

00:23:15.880 --> 00:23:18.160
creative act itself, then you're probably
doing the wrong thing.

00:23:18.160 --> 00:23:19.350
- Then it's not sustainable, I would say.

00:23:19.350 --> 00:23:24.530
It's either not sustainable or it's not good,
and the quality is not there, I would say.

00:23:24.530 --> 00:23:28.060
One thing I believe is it's really important
to define your own success.

00:23:28.060 --> 00:23:33.430
It's really important to decide for yourself
what success looks like as opposed to letting

00:23:33.430 --> 00:23:36.400
someone else imposed their definition of success
upon you.

00:23:36.400 --> 00:23:40.150
So whenever you begin a big project, if you're
doing something on your own, if you're starting

00:23:40.150 --> 00:23:44.550
a business or a blog or going back to school
or even if it's an exercise program, whatever

00:23:44.550 --> 00:23:47.430
it is, you should have your own definition
of success.

00:23:47.430 --> 00:23:51.300
I think maybe if we go back to somebody who's
in that beginning part of their career, I

00:23:51.300 --> 00:23:54.940
think the wrong advice to them would be, say,
just pick one of those things, and that's

00:23:54.940 --> 00:23:56.480
what everybody says all the time.

00:23:56.480 --> 00:23:58.380
They're always like, "Oh you have to focus.

00:23:58.380 --> 00:24:00.360
"You have to just pick your niche or your
niche.

00:24:00.360 --> 00:24:05.010
"You got to do that," and how are you really
going to find that combination of factors

00:24:05.010 --> 00:24:09.020
of joy, money, and flow without expanding
your options, without trying a lot of different

00:24:09.020 --> 00:24:10.020
stuff.

00:24:10.020 --> 00:24:11.020
So you did that, you did that in your career.

00:24:11.020 --> 00:24:15.930
I did that, and I think in the beginning part
of really trying to figure out what am I good

00:24:15.930 --> 00:24:21.670
at, and also not just what am I good at but
what is going to be rewarded in the marketplace,

00:24:21.670 --> 00:24:25.520
I said yes to a lot of different things, and
so I always encourage people when you're starting

00:24:25.520 --> 00:24:26.520
out, expand your options.

00:24:26.520 --> 00:24:31.370
Do everything that you can to have a lot of
different experiences, and then as you become

00:24:31.370 --> 00:24:36.010
more successful, as you have more experience,
then you limit the options, then you start

00:24:36.010 --> 00:24:39.840
being more selective, then you start being
like okay, I am going to apply that litmus

00:24:39.840 --> 00:24:41.110
test more often.

00:24:41.110 --> 00:24:45.680
I am going to say okay, I've got all these
different things I could do.

00:24:45.680 --> 00:24:48.150
What is most aligned?

00:24:48.150 --> 00:24:50.720
Where can I have the most convergence?

00:24:50.720 --> 00:24:52.100
What am I really like getting close to?

00:24:52.100 --> 00:24:53.780
How am I going to have the biggest impact
in the world?

00:24:53.780 --> 00:24:56.500
But I don't think you can necessarily do that
when you're just getting started because you

00:24:56.500 --> 00:24:56.530
